this is Hercules he's supposed to be  walking in a nice straight line but  actually he's just monkeying around  Hercules is one of two chimps helping  scientists understand the origins of  human walking this is what humans look  like when they walk to keep track of the  body's movements research has stuck  markers on specific points of their  subjects and trace their position and  this is Hercules his colleague Leo  taking a stroll clearly pretty different  his human counterpart chimps swing their  hips much more than humans when they  walk by tracing the markers the  researchers were able to analyze these  differences when humans walk we swing  our ribs in the opposite direction to  our hips which helps us to save energy  from looking at their skeletons chimps  torsos appear to be too rigid to make  this movement possible but surprisingly  Hercules and Leo's bodies do twist a  little as they walk you can see their  ribs move in the same direction as their  hips but they are cancelling out some of  the motion these two walking styles may  still seem pretty different but if you  hold the hip still you can see that the  ribs are still compensating by the same  amount in both humans and chimps these  chimp results imply that human ancestors  like Australopithecus might also have  walked more dynamically than we  previously thought who would have  thought that Hercules strutting his  stuff on a catwalk could teach us so  much  you
 

